{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>For a hand is upon the throne of Yah</b> — The hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, was raised to swear by His throne, that there shall be war and eternal enmity against Amalek. And why is it written כֵּס (kes, \"throne\") and not כִּסֵּא (kisse, \"throne\")? And moreover, the Divine Name is split in half — the Holy One, blessed be He, swore that the throne is not whole and the Name is not complete until the name of Amalek son of Esau is utterly blotted out, and once his name is blotted out the Name of HaShem will be complete and the throne whole, as it is written (Psalms 9:7), \"The enemy are destroyed, ruined forever\" — this is Esau, as it is written, \"and his wrath he guarded forever\" (Amos 1:11); \"their memory is perished, they are no more\" (Psalms 9:7) — what is written afterward? \"But HaShem sits forever\" (v. 8) — behold, the Name is complete; \"He has established His throne for judgment\" (v. 8) — behold, the throne is whole. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 17:16). And it is a midrash of the Sages (Tanḥuma, Ki Teitzei 11). And some explain: for whenever a hand is upon the throne of HaShem, there shall be war for HaShem against Amalek, and so it shall be from generation to generation. The meaning is: for whenever there is a king in Israel sitting upon the throne of HaShem, he shall wage war against Amalek — and this is an allusion to Saul the first king, and so from generation to generation, meaning that every king in Israel is obligated to wage war against them until they are blotted out. And this too is a midrash of the Gemara, for they said (Sanhedrin 20b): since it says <b>for a hand is upon the throne of Yah, war for HaShem against Amalek from generation to generation</b> — this teaches that a king must first be appointed over them, and \"the throne of Yah\" means nothing other than a king, as it is written (I Chronicles 29:23), \"and Solomon sat\" — as is found in the chapter Kohen Gadol (Sanhedrin 20b). And according to the plain sense, this is correct.<br>And by way of truth: the hand that is upon the throne of Yah, and which constitutes war for HaShem — it came upon Amalek from generation to generation, for the attribute of judgment above shall be upon him to blot him out forever, generation after generation. And the midrash of the Sages (Tanḥuma, Ki Teitzei 15) concerning the complete Name and the whole throne alludes to this. The reason for the punishment that Amalek was punished more than all the other nations is because all the nations heard and trembled (Exodus 15:14), and Philistia, Edom, Moab, and the inhabitants of Canaan melted away (ibid.) because of the fear of HaShem and the splendor of His glory, whereas Amalek came from afar as one who presumes to prevail against the Name. Therefore it is said of him, \"and he did not fear Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 25:18). And furthermore, because he is a descendant of Esau and kin to us — one who passes by and involves himself in a quarrel that is not his."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The comment covers the full verse lemma and its midrashic and kabbalistic interpretations; Ramban's 'וְעַל דֶּרֶךְ הָאֱמֶת' passage is rendered literally and left cryptic per the voice rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:10:08.293430522Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}